sdme (0.22.0-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Add ephemeral containers. --ephemeral on sdme new and sdme create runs
    the container as a transient systemd unit, sdme-ephemeral@NAME.service,
    and any stop removes it with its root, its state and everything nspawn
    set up. sdme new ends it when its session ends: the shell exits, the
    command finishes, or the client dies. sdme create starts it detached.
    --main-service UNIT, the OCI app by default, ends it when that unit
    finishes, and --health-cmd, --health-http and --health-tcp end it after
    --health-retries failed checks run from the host. restart, enable and
    set refuse ephemeral containers, and sdme prune lists one left behind by
    a host crash.
  * Hide host users' systemd user units from host-rootfs containers.
    /etc/systemd/user joins the default host_rootfs_opaque_dirs, and each
    home's ~/.config/systemd/user and ~/.config/systemd/user.control are
    made opaque at create time, keeping the home's owner and mode, so the
    host's user units no longer start in the container when a user session
    begins there. The dbus.service alias is kept in both unit directories,
    and a host clone now accepts an absolute alias target, which gives host
    clones on Fedora-family hosts their system bus.
  * Accept a unique prefix of a pod name, as for container names, in pod rm,
    pod net attach and detach, and --pod and --oci-pod, which store the full
    name. A missing pod is reported as "no pod found matching".
  * Let a start right after an unclean exit wait for the previous instance
    to stop. nspawn refused it when the old unit was still deactivating,
    because the leftover unix-export mount was not reclaimed yet.
  * Document ephemeral containers in the architecture guide and a new
    tutorial, and mention pod name prefixes in the pod networking tutorial.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:44:41 -0700

sdme (0.21.1-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Keep permission bits, modification times and device numbers in tar
    exports. sdme fs export to a tarball wrote every entry as 0755 or 0644
    with an mtime of 2006-07-24, so an exported rootfs lost its setuid,
    setgid and sticky bits and had its 0600 and 0640 files widened to 0644.
  * sdme config set accepts userns_nested_ranges, restart_sec, auto_fs_gc,
    default_export_free_space and default_create_masked_services. It
    rejected them as unknown keys although config get prints them. The
    config help lists userns_nested_ranges and restart_sec.
  * Correct the error of a create that is rejected inside a user-namespaced
    container. mknod of a device node needs CAP_MKNOD in the initial user
    namespace, so the syscall filter of the outer container does not matter;
    the error names what works instead: create on the host, or in an outer
    container without --userns, --hardened or --strict.
  * Correct the note printed when a btrfs subvolume is parked in .trash:
    sdme prune on the same data root destroys it once the mount has
    user_subvol_rm_allowed. Prune on the host does not scan a data root
    inside a container.
  * The embedded AI skill no longer says that --userns-nested lets a
    container host nested workloads, and drops --capability CAP_NET_ADMIN
    from the container engine flags; --network-veth already grants it.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Mon, 05 Oct 2026 11:01:22 +0000

sdme (0.21.0-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Give new overlay --userns containers an idmapped lower layer instead of
    pre-chowning the rootfs into the upper layer. The unit bind-mounts the lower
    layer read-only and idmapped under /run/sdme/idmap, mounts the overlay on
    it, and drops the bind. On a 228M rootfs, create --userns goes from 1.9
    seconds and a 235M upper layer to 0.01 seconds and 32K. The pre-chown
    remains the fallback where the lower layer cannot be idmapped (util-linux
    older than 2.39, a filesystem without idmapped mounts, sdme inside a user
    namespace), and for existing containers.
  * Add the userns_idmap_lower config key; set it to no to always pre-chown.
  * File capabilities in the rootfs are preserved and honoured with an idmapped
    lower layer. A binary whose effective file capabilities are outside the
    container's bounding set, such as ping under --hardened, fails to execute.
    The pre-chown loses them.
  * Shift what sdme cp writes into a stopped container that has an idmapped
    lower layer, so it is owned by the container's root and not by nobody.
  * Host-rootfs containers boot with --userns. With the pre-chown, the create
    failed in the overlay's own work directory.
  * Refuse to start a container with an idmapped lower layer whose UID base is
    in use by another running machine.
  * Reject --oci-pod together with --userns, --hardened or --strict, in create,
    new and the kube commands. Inside a user namespace the app could not enter
    the pod's network namespace, and systemd started it outside the pod without
    an error. Use --pod, or --oci-pod with --private-network.
  * Mark a create that never finished. A create that is killed leaves
    INCOMPLETE=yes in the state file: sdme ps reports it, sdme start refuses it,
    and sdme rm removes it, also when the container directory is missing.
  * sdme rm removes the temp files that killed state writes left behind, and
    sdme ps no longer lists them as containers.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 12:52:00 +0000

sdme (0.20.1-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Wait out an OCI app's stop timeout when its container is stopped or
    removed: the pod's terminationGracePeriodSeconds for a kube pod, 90
    seconds for an OCI app outside one. `sdme kube delete` on a running pod
    whose workload ignores SIGTERM timed out after 30 seconds and left the pod
    and its rootfs behind, and `sdme stop` failed the same way.
  * Give the container unit a stop timeout longer than the guest's when the
    app's stop timeout is 90 seconds or more. The host used to kill such a
    container after 90 seconds, which cut a longer grace period short and left
    the unit in failed state.
  * Honor stop_timeout_terminate in `sdme rm` and `sdme fs build`, which
    waited a fixed 30 seconds.
  * Queue concurrent `sdme create --userns` calls on the UID range lock
    instead of failing with "cannot lock userns/shift", and reserve the range
    before the lock is released, so two containers cannot be given the same
    one.
  * Remove the container again when `sdme create` or `sdme new` fails after
    creating it.
  * Replace unit files by rename instead of rewriting them in place. A start
    that loaded the shared template unit in the middle of a rewrite was
    refused as masked. A per-start -t now goes into the container's own
    drop-in, and the template follows the boot_timeout setting.
  * Reclaim a leftover unix-export mount under /run/systemd/nspawn/unix-export
    as well. On systemd 259 a container that had been killed could not be
    started again until a second attempt.
  * Fail a start at once, with the unit's journal, when the unit dies before
    systemd-nspawn registers the machine. It used to wait for the boot
    timeout.
  * Show the cause of a failed operation in the batch commands (start, stop,
    rm, enable, restart, pod rm, fs rm).
  * Find a leftover pod rootfs inside an unmounted btrfs loopback pool when
    `sdme kube create` uses the overlay backend.
  * Skip files that disappear from the rootfs while `sdme create --userns`
    shifts ownership, instead of failing the create.
  * Use a separate temp file for each atomic write, so concurrent writers of
    one state file cannot publish a truncated file.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 07:33:48 +0000

sdme (0.20.0-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Stop `sdme kube apply` and `sdme kube create` before any copy or pull
    when a kube-<pod> rootfs exists that no container claims, and name the
    command that removes it. The create previously ran to the end and failed
    at the final rename with "Directory not empty". Removing a pod with
    `sdme rm` is enough to leave such a rootfs behind.
  * The btrfs backend no longer deletes a leftover pod rootfs silently; both
    backends now refuse and report it.
  * Let `sdme kube delete` remove a leftover pod rootfs when no container of
    that name exists, on either backend, and succeed when nothing is left. It
    previously failed with "container not found". It now also refuses to
    remove a rootfs that another container references.
  * Remove the pod rootfs again when container creation fails after the
    rootfs was built.
  * Emit RequiresMountsFor for the btrfs loopback pool in the [Unit] section
    of container drop-ins, so systemd pulls in the mount before nspawn
    starts. Recognized legacy drop-ins are repaired on package upgrade and by
    root sdme commands; the repair is idempotent and does not restart
    containers.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 11:06:21 +0000

sdme (0.19.1-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Build the static musl release binaries again. The contained copy engine
    reached mount identity through the libc statx wrapper, which libc gates
    behind a cfg that musl targets do not get, so both release targets failed
    to compile. Call the statx syscall directly with in-crate kernel types,
    which needs no statx symbol from the target libc.
  * Cross-compile both musl release targets in CI on every push, so this class
    of breakage fails before a release tag rather than after one.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:05:00 +0100

sdme (0.19.0-1~resolute1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Route `sdme cp` and `sdme fs build` COPY through a descriptor-relative
    copy engine that pins the write root and every destination directory,
    rejects ancestor symlinks, and publishes only completed inodes. Symlinks
    and other special nodes now publish on live container roots as well, so
    build COPY can copy /etc/os-release and directories containing symlinks.
  * Reclaim leftover systemd-nspawn runtime state at
    /run/systemd/nspawn/<name> on container start and removal, after
    confirming through both systemd and machined that nothing of that name is
    running. A container killed rather than stopped cleanly previously blocked
    the next start of the same name.
  * Default Kubernetes terminationGracePeriodSeconds to 30 seconds as the spec
    requires. Pods that omitted the field previously inherited systemd's 90
    second stop timeout.
  * Make forced rootfs replacement atomic and crash-recoverable, refuse
    replacing a rootfs referenced by any container, and require inspection of
    partial replacement artifacts before a later import or removal proceeds.
  * Refuse container removal when D-Bus cannot confirm unit state.
  * Validate OCI digests before deriving cache paths, keep layer download
    scratch outside the extracted tree, confine OCI app setup writes to the
    staging tree, and validate app ancestry before moving image entries.
  * Refresh dependencies and replace serde_yml.
  * Move reference documentation and tutorials to docs/ as plain Markdown
    shared by GitHub and the website.

 -- Alexandre Fiori <fiorix@gmail.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:17:21 +0100

